  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Defining Core Paths**\n\nI\'m focused on developing the \"Bharat Bridge\" app, aiming for a cleaner alignment with its intended purpose. The current refinement centers on core user paths, specifically separating roles for \"Speaker\" and \"Listener\" from the outset. I am currently concentrating on the implementation of these distinct pathways, for improved usability. I am considering several methods of ensuring proper access to only the necessary features and information, with the intention of making the UI very clear and intuitive.\n\n\n**Refining Language Support**\n\nI\'m now prioritizing the handling of per-listener language preferences. The current system provides a single target language per session, set by the speaker. My focus is on adjusting the architecture to allow each listener to receive translations in their chosen language, which raises efficiency concerns when considering a scale of 1000+ listeners. I need to figure out how to generate the translations without significant latency. I\'m investigating real-time WebSocket architecture and integration with the Gemini Live API.\n\n\n**Analyzing Translation Architecture**\n\nI\'m now deep-diving into the architectural implications of supporting per-listener language preferences. My previous approach, using Gemini Live with a single target language, won\'t scale. I\'ve re-evaluated the roles: the speaker broadcasts, and the server handles translation. If a thousand listeners want ten languages, I need ten translation streams. The \"Multimodal\" API poses a challenge as it supports only one target language per connection. I need a new approach. I\'m exploring how to pass each listener\'s `targetLang` to the Gemini Live API efficiently upon connection.\n\n\n**Restructuring Server Logic**\n\nI\'m now revising the server\'s translation architecture to efficiently manage per-listener language preferences. My key shift is from a 1:1 session-to-Gemini mapping to a 1:N setup, where each session supports multiple languages. A crucial aspect is grouping listeners by their preferred language; the speaker\'s audio feeds into a unique Gemini Live session for each language. This approach minimizes latency while ensuring proper translation for each listener, and I\'m currently assessing the existing code structure in `server.ts`, specifically how the `sessions` Map can accommodate the new structure. I\'m also considering the implications for saving translated text when multiple languages are present.\n\n\n**Implementing Data Persistence**\n\nI\'m now focusing on how to save translated data effectively, as the expert user suggested. I\'m ensuring all four data types: source text, source audio, target text, and target audio are saved, and I\'m updating `server.ts` to manage multiple target languages per speaker. I need to handle a pool of Gemini Live sessions for each session ID, grouped by target language. Listeners are added to language groups, and a new session is created if one doesn\'t exist.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 5
      nanos: 929733242
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
