  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Refining Language Preference**\n\nI\'m currently updating the `joinLiveSession` function. The key change involves using the user\'s `targetLang` for the session instead of defaulting to the session\'s language, to personalize the experience. This change will affect how users are connected. I have modified the websocket message to reflect this change.\n\n\n**Considering Data Storage Strategy**\n\nI\'m now grappling with data storage for transcriptions and translations. The current approach of each speaker handling message pushing is inefficient, especially with multiple languages. Saving the original text and an \"official\" translation appears insufficient for Bharat Bridge\'s multi-lingual needs. I\'m leaning toward a schema update to store the speaker\'s original text along with the specific Gemini-generated translation for each user, allowing for a more complete and accessible history of the session in multiple languages.\n\n\n**Prioritizing Data Persistence**\n\nI\'m now focusing on how to persist transcriptions, given the user\'s explicit requirement. I\'m moving away from exclusively server-side saves, considering the need for individual language preferences and the initial complexity. I\'m exploring an approach where listeners have the option to save their preferred translations, which aligns with the need to save personalized transcripts. I\'ll prioritize updating `App.tsx` and adding a \"Bharat Bridge\" mode.\n\n\n**Integrating Bharat Bridge Purpose**\n\nI\'m now integrating \"Bharat Bridge\" directly into the central UI, making it the primary focus. I\'ve updated `App.tsx` to reflect this change, using a `useState` hook for app mode. I\'ve updated the display in the main central block to offer \"Bharat Bridge\" controls upfront. I\'m focusing on the main view\'s role selection to align with the user\'s vision. Now, I\'m adjusting the `onmessage` function.\n\n\n**Addressing Translation Display**\n\nI\'m now focusing on the difference between live and historical messages. The current issue lies in `App.tsx` where the `messages` state doesn\'t properly handle `targetLang`. I\'m working to separate real-time (WebSocket) messages into a `liveMessages` state. I\'m considering showing historical messages from Firestore separately or combining them. I\'m evaluating the tradeoffs of storing all unique translations versus sticking with the speaker\'s default for history.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 14
      nanos: 758051864
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
